Using NTAG215 data sheet to find these.

GET_VERSION:
    Retrieve information on NTAG family, version, storage size, etc.

    RESPONSE for NTAG 215 will be 8 bytes.
    byte 0-7:
    header, vendor ID, product type, subtype, major version, minor version, storage size, protocol type.

    CMD code: 60h
    Arguments: NONE


READ:
    Requires start page address, returns 16 bytes of four NTAG215 pages.
    Example: address is 03h then pages 03h, 04h, 05h, 06h are returned.
        Special conditions apply if address is near the end of memory.
        Also apply if at least part of the address is within password protected area.

    In initial state, all memory pages are allowed as Addr parameter.
    (00h to 86h for NTAG215).
    Addressing beyond limits results in NAK response. 
    Also roll-over will occur,
    i.e. 85h, 86h, 00h, 01h
    
    CMD code: 30h
    Arguments: Addr (1 byte)


FAST_READ:
    Requires start page address and end page address.
    Returns all n*4 bytes of the addressed pages.
    Example: start address 03h and end address 07h then pages
    03h, 04h, 05h, 06h, and 07h are returned.

    CMD code: 3Ah
    Arguments: StartAddr (1 byte), EndAddr (1 byte)

    NOTE: Receive buffer of NFC device must be able to handle the requested amount of data. This command CAN read out whole memory.


WRITE:
    Requires a block address, and writes 4 bytes of data into the addressed page.

    In initial state valid Addr prameters are as follows:
    (02h to 86h)
    Pages which are locked against writing cannot be reprogrammed.

    CMD code: A2h
    Arguments: Addr (1 byte), Data to write (4 bytes)
    ex: { 0xA2, 0x14, 0x73, 0x65, 0x6e, 0x67}
    --> { write, addr, 's', 'e', 'n', 'g' }


